# Trivy ignore file - best practices
#
# Keep this file minimal. Only ignore vulnerabilities that are:
#  - identified as false positives, or
#  - accepted risk with a documented mitigation plan, or
#  - in third‑party/proprietary software you cannot patch.
#
# For each ignored item include: author, reason and an expiry/review date so ignores are re-evaluated.
# Format is simple: a vulnerability ID per line (e.g. CVE-YYYY-NNNN). Inline comments are allowed.

# Example (replace placeholders with real values):
# CVE-YYYY-NNNNN  # author: alice@example.com  reason: false positive in build artifact  expires: 2026-01-01

# --- Your ignores below (only add after proper review) ---
# Add a single CVE per line, and annotate with reason and expiry as a comment.
# CVE-PLACEHOLDER-1  # author: you@example.com  reason: <explain why>  expires: YYYY-MM-DD
# CVE-PLACEHOLDER-2  # author: you@example.com  reason: <explain why>  expires: YYYY-MM-DD

# --- Image / package scoped ignores (use very sparingly) ---
# If you need to scope an ignore to a specific image or package, document it here.
# Note: Trivy's legacy .trivyignore accepts vulnerability IDs. For finer scoping, prefer
# per-repo documentation linking the CVE to the affected package/image and keep the global
# .trivyignore small.

# --- Maintenance notes ---
# - Review this file regularly (e.g. at each release or quarterly).
# - Remove entries once a patch is available or the issue is no longer relevant.
# - Prefer fixing/patching over ignoring vulnerabilities.